<!-- cell: title-page id="39262127-a15b-45ca-9295-70c2372cdc6b" title="Plot 14" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="8518376e-c997-4186-ab82-35faa73bc74d" -->

Jo Harland took on Plot 14 at the Ashley Vale allotments the week after she cried in the stationery cupboard at work. She had not meant to cry. She had gone in for a stapler, and the stapler had been the last straw in a quarter of fourteen-hour days, three product launches and a performance review that described her as a natural multitasker.

Plot 14 was a rectangle of nettles and broken cloches between a rhubarb patch and a shed with a sign that said OKAFOR — KNOCK. She cleared it in two furious weekends, bought every seed packet in the garden centre, and planted the lot in one afternoon, in rows as straight as a spreadsheet.

Nothing came up.

Or rather, everything came up at once, crowded and pale, and then the slugs came, and then a week of rain, and by June her tomato seedlings lay flat on the soil like tiny drowned soldiers.

Mr. Okafor knocked on her shed, which she felt was against the spirit of his sign. He was a retired bus driver of seventy-four in a straw hat, and he had been growing runner beans on the next plot since before she was born.

"You plant like you're late for something," he said.

"I am late for something. I'm always late for something."

He looked at her for a long moment. Then he handed her a single tomato seedling in a yoghurt pot. "One," he said. "Water it in the evening. Don't touch it in between. Come and tell me in August."

She did as she was told, partly to prove him wrong. She came on Sunday mornings with a flask of coffee and sat on an upturned bucket and watched one plant do almost nothing, very slowly. Her phone stayed in the car. Some Sundays she forgot it was there.

In August the plant was taller than she was, and hung with fruit so red it looked painted. She picked the first tomato and ate it standing up, warm from the sun, and it tasted of nothing she had ever bought.

The next morning she asked her boss for a four-day week. He said yes so quickly she wondered why she had never asked.

She brought Mr. Okafor a bowl of tomatoes. He took one, bit into it, and nodded. "August," he said, as if it had been her idea all along.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b6a54b3e-8faf-4fe4-9005-51bb4938aa7e" title="Jo Harland" words="0-21, 257-267, 324-346" -->

A burnt-out marketing manager who takes on an allotment after crying in the stationery cupboard at work.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="ea27c050-ce8c-4af6-b4f4-4c5a3631eacf" title="Mr. Okafor" words="171-198, 199-207, 378-385" -->

A retired bus driver who has grown runner beans on the next plot for decades and teaches Jo to wait.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="97f72b46-72ea-47bc-8d42-a3d08e901943" title="The Ashley Vale allotments" words="63-86, 268-292" -->

The allotments where Jo's Plot 14 lies between a rhubarb patch and Mr. Okafor's shed.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="89cea694-4e86-4261-af40-5deacd5afc8a" title="Burnout" words="28-62, 208-217, 347-357" -->

Jo's exhaustion from fourteen-hour days and constant lateness, which she carries into her gardening.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="552942dc-3d71-426d-b6a4-d2e2ff590722" title="Patience" words="238-256, 293-305, 386-397" -->

The slow work of letting one plant grow, which changes how Jo lives.

<!-- cell: plot id="e1e1dc2a-d08d-4c24-8275-f24428d39e19" title="Plot 14" words="0-21, 87-115, 119-154, 155-170, 199-207, 226-256, 268-292, 306-323, 347-357" -->

Jo plants everything at once and loses it all; Mr. Okafor gives her a single seedling and tells her to wait; by August it fruits, and she asks for a four-day week.
